\section{Kinds}{

The kind is set by the class of \code{x}: an \code{sf} object is \code{geospatial}, a
\code{SpatRaster} is a \code{raster}, any other data frame is a \code{table}.
\itemize{
\item Geospatial data get the columns \code{.rcs_id} (row identifier), \code{h3} (the
H3 cell at \code{h3_res} holding the largest part of each geometry; not with
\code{geocode = FALSE}) and \code{bbox} (bounding box), and are sorted by \code{h3}.
Rows that cross H3 cells are listed in an index table
\verb{<dataset>__h3}, written, replaced and removed with the dataset.
\item Prepared geospatial data (\code{\link[=rcs_geo_add]{rcs_geo_add()}}, or rows read back with
\code{collect()}) may hold these columns already: \code{.rcs_id} is dropped and
assigned again; \code{h3} and \code{bbox} must equal what the write computes
(else the write aborts naming the rows; prepare with the same
\code{geocode_engine}, or call \code{\link[=rcs_geo_strip]{rcs_geo_strip()}} first), and \code{h3_res}, when
not given, is taken from the cells. Columns \verb{h3_p<r>} are written as
they are; with \code{partition = "h3"} one named as the chosen partition
column must hold the same cells, and then serves as it. Raw and
prepared data give the same version.
\item A raster is written as one Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG), the only
raster format; it cannot be partitioned or updated (see "Rasters").
\item A lazy table on a DuckDB connection is a \code{table}, or \code{geospatial} when
it has a column \code{geometry} (see "DuckDB input").
}
}

\section{Rasters}{

A \code{SpatRaster} is written as one Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF (COG), the only
raster format: a reader can fetch a part of it without the rest, also
from a bucket. The raster needs a CRS, cell values and unique layer
names.

\strong{What is stored where.} The file holds the cell values only; for a
class map, the codes. What describes them is recorded per layer in the
catalog and in the dataset's manifest: the data type, the NoData value
(\code{nodata}), the resolution and the dimensions (\code{resolution}, \code{dim}), the
legend of a categorical layer (\code{values}, \code{levels}, \code{levels_name}) and
its colours (\code{color_values}, \code{colors}). \code{\link[=store_read]{store_read()}} returns the
raster with its categories and colours set again, and
\code{\link[=store_describe]{store_describe()}} shows the record. No side file (\code{.aux.xml},
\code{.aux.json}) is written.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Data type} (\code{datatype}). All layers get one type, as a GeoTIFF
requires. With \code{datatype = NULL}, the first rule that applies:
\enumerate{
\item A raster read from files, with its cells still on disk, keeps the
type of its source: a Byte map stays \code{INT1U}, an \code{FLT4S} file
stays \code{FLT4S}. No pass over the cells is needed. This rule is
skipped when the layers have different types, and for a raster
with a scale or an offset (in the file, or set with
\code{terra::scoff()}), whose values are not the stored numbers.
\item Whole numbers get the smallest of \code{INT1U}, \code{INT2U}, \code{INT2S},
\code{INT4U}, \code{INT4S} that holds the values and the NoData value: codes
1 to 5 with \code{nodata = 0} take one byte per cell. Whole numbers
are integer layers (\code{terra::is.int()}) and categorical layers;
doubles that happen to be whole are not, so convert them with
\code{terra::as.int()} first.
\item Anything else is \code{FLT8S}. Doubles are never narrowed to \code{FLT4S}
unless \code{datatype = "FLT4S"} asks for it (it rounds them to single
precision).
}

A \code{datatype} given is used as it is. An integer type needs whole
numbers inside its range, else the write aborts.
\item \strong{NoData} (\code{nodata}). One value marks the missing cells of all layers.
Cells that hold this value are missing when read back; this is how a
map whose \code{0} means "outside the map" is written (\code{nodata = 0}). With
\code{nodata = NULL}, the first rule that applies:
\enumerate{
\item A flag set in the session on a raster read from files
(\verb{terra::NAflag(x) <- }). On a raster in memory terra applies the
flag at once, the cells become missing, and no flag is left: the
rules below then apply.
\item The NoData tag of the source file. It is not used for a layer
with a scale or an offset, whose values are not the stored
numbers the tag refers to.
\item For an integer type chosen from the values (rule 2 of "Data
type"), the default of the type: \code{255} for \code{INT1U}, \code{65535} for
\code{INT2U}, \code{-32768} for \code{INT2S}, \code{4294967295} for \code{INT4U},
\code{-2147483648} for \code{INT4S}. The type is chosen so that no cell
holds that value: values up to 254 fit \code{INT1U}, and with 255
among them the type is \code{INT2U}.
\item For an integer type kept from the source or given with
\code{datatype}, the same default, but only when cells are missing
(found by one pass over the cells, made only in this case). The
write aborts when a cell holds that value; give \code{nodata} then.
Without missing cells the file has no NoData value: a Byte file
without a tag whose \code{0} means "outside the map" gets none unless
\code{nodata = 0} is given.
\item A float type has none: its missing cells are \code{NaN}.
}

Layers whose flags or tags differ abort and ask for \code{nodata}. A
\code{nodata} that the data type cannot hold aborts.
\item \strong{Creation options} (\code{gdal}). The defaults suit reads from a bucket:
\code{COMPRESS=DEFLATE}, \code{PREDICTOR=2} (integer types) or \code{PREDICTOR=3}
(float types), \code{BLOCKSIZE=512}, \code{BIGTIFF=IF_SAFER}, and overviews
built anew, never copied from a source file
(\code{OVERVIEWS=IGNORE_EXISTING}), with \code{OVERVIEW_RESAMPLING=NEAREST}
(integer types) or \code{AVERAGE} (float types). \code{gdal} is a character
vector of \code{KEY=VALUE} options of GDAL's COG driver, e.g.
\code{gdal = c("COMPRESS=ZSTD", "LEVEL=9")}: a key that has a default
replaces it, other keys are added. Keys are compared in upper case, a
key given twice aborts, and the order given does not change the file.
The options are kept in the file, not in the catalog.
\item \strong{Legend of a class map.} A categorical layer is one with a category
table, set with \code{levels(x) <- data.frame(value = , label = )}: the
codes in the first column, their labels in the second. \verb{levels<-}
renames the layer to the label column, so set \code{names(x)} after it.
The codes are written to the file; the legend is recorded as \code{values}
(the codes, sorted), \code{levels} (their labels) and \code{levels_name} (the
name of the label column). Codes must be whole numbers and every code
needs a label, else the write aborts. Of a category table with
several label columns, only the active one (\code{terra::activeCat()}) is
kept; the others are dropped, with a message.
\item \strong{Colours.} The colour table of a layer, set with
\code{terra::coltab(x) <- data.frame(value = , col = )}, is recorded as
\code{color_values} (the codes, sorted) and \code{colors} (\verb{#RRGGBBAA}). With a
legend, only the colours of the legend's codes are kept. Without a
legend every row of the colour table is kept; a colour table read
from a file has 256 rows (the codes 0 to 255; a code without a colour
is black, or transparent when it is the file's NoData value), and all
256 are recorded. Colours come from the raster
only: a style file such as a QGIS \code{.qml} is not read.
\item \strong{Not stored.} The time and the units of layers (\code{terra::time()},
\code{terra::units()}), and the columns of a category table other than the
codes and the active labels. The file is written without categories,
colour table, time and units; \code{x} itself is never changed. Only other
metadata that a source file holds itself may be copied with it when
GDAL copies the file (next point).
\item \strong{Two ways to write, and the room they need.} The file is first
written on the local disk, in the staging folder
\code{getOption("rcs.staging_dir")} (default \code{\link[=tempdir]{tempdir()}}), and then
uploaded. Every temporary file lies below that folder.
\itemize{
\item A raster opened with \code{terra::rast(file)} and not changed in the
session is copied by GDAL straight from its file, with no
intermediate file. On a simulated map of 25,000 x 25,000 cells
the staging folder held at most about 1.4 times the size of the
output. "Not changed" means: all layers of one file in their
order, with the file's layer names, extent and CRS, and no NoData
flag or tag other than the value written. Setting a legend (and
the layer name again) or colours is not a change. A file with a
scale or an offset is not copied by GDAL.
\item Any other raster is written by terra: one in memory, computed,
cropped, renamed, a subset of the layers, from several files,
with a scale or an offset, or with another CRS or NoData value
than its file. For a large raster terra first writes an
intermediate file; on the same map the staging folder then held
about 3.6 times the size of the output. A raster held in memory
also needs RAM for all its cells, so write a large map from its
file.
}

For a raster read from files, the write warns (class
\code{rcs_warning_staging_space}) when the drive of the staging folder has
less free room than twice the size of the source files, and goes on.
The ps package tells the free room; without it, and for a raster in
memory, there is no check. \code{options(rcs.staging_dir = )} moves the
staging to another drive.
}

The same raster written with the same arguments gives the same file and
the same version. The legend, the colours, the time and the units do
not change the file: the same cells with and without them give the same
version, and only the catalog entry differs. Writing the same cells
with another legend or other colours keeps the version, sends no file
and replaces the legend and the colours recorded for that version; the
earlier ones are not kept. A \code{datatype}, \code{nodata} or \code{gdal} gives
another file and another version only when it changes the file:
\code{nodata = 0} for a source file already tagged \code{0}, or an option equal
to its default, gives the same one. The two ways of writing may give
different bytes for the same cells. A raster written with a version of
the package before \verb{0.0.0.9005} gets a new version when it is written
again, even with unchanged cells: the bytes of the file differ.
}

\section{DuckDB input}{

\code{x} may be a lazy table on a DuckDB connection (from \code{\link[dplyr:tbl]{dplyr::tbl()}}, for
example on a view of \code{\link[=store_duckdb]{store_duckdb()}}, or from
\code{store_open(engine = "duckdb")}), so that a result computed in DuckDB is
stored without being collected into R as a whole.
\itemize{
\item A table (no \code{geometry} column) with a \code{partition} is written one
partition at a time. The query runs as a subquery \code{2 + P} times (P
partitions): once to check the key, once for the partition values and
row counts, then once per partition, whose rows are copied into the
temporary table \verb{rcs_write_<random>_part}, written, checked and
dropped. DuckDB then needs room for about the largest partition, not
for the whole result. The query must therefore be deterministic (give
the same rows each time), and is best a scan of stored files or views;
an expensive query is better computed once into a DuckDB table first.
\code{options(rcs.duckdb_stream = FALSE)} turns this off.
\item Otherwise (no partition, geospatial data, or
\code{rcs.duckdb_stream = FALSE}) the query runs once into a temporary copy,
the table \verb{rcs_write_<random>} on the same connection, which every
later step reads; the copy needs room for the whole result in DuckDB's
memory or its temporary folder.
}

The temporary tables are dropped when the call ends, also after an error.
The key and the partitions are checked in DuckDB, and the rows are
fetched in batches of \code{getOption("rcs.duckdb_batch_rows")} rows (default
\code{5e5}), so that R holds about one batch at a time; a progress bar shows
the batches unless \code{options(rcs.quiet = TRUE)}. For the duration of the
call the connection runs with \code{preserve_insertion_order = true}, so that
the rows reach R in the order the write asks for; the caller's setting is
restored when the call ends, also after an error. DuckDB never uploads:
the files are staged and land in the locations as for any other write
(every location, unless the dataset is kept in the primary only).
Staged files need free room equal to the output in
\code{getOption("rcs.staging_dir")} (default \code{\link[=tempdir]{tempdir()}}); setting it to a
folder on the store's drive avoids copying them across drives. The files
equal those written from the same rows collected into R, so both give the
same version.
\itemize{
\item Modes \code{"create"} and \code{"replace"} only; \code{"update"} aborts (collect the
rows and update from R, or write the whole dataset).
\item Columns \code{.rcs_id}, \code{h3}, \code{bbox} and \code{geom} are the package's own: they
are dropped with a message and computed again. Columns \verb{h3_p<r>} are
user columns.
\item Types: integer, double, \code{VARCHAR}, \code{DATE} and \code{BOOLEAN} are stored as
they are; \code{BIGINT} is stored as 64-bit integers read back as
\code{bit64::integer64}; \code{TIMESTAMP} is stored without time zone (its wall
clock) and \verb{TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE} in UTC. \code{HUGEINT}, \code{UHUGEINT},
\code{INTERVAL}, \code{UNION} and \code{MAP} columns abort, naming them; cast them in
the query. DuckDB's \code{sum()} of an integer column is a \code{HUGEINT}: write
\code{as.integer(sum(x))} or \code{as.numeric(sum(x))} in dbplyr. Values keep
their storage types: a factor read through a view is \code{VARCHAR} and is
stored as text.
\item Geospatial data: a column \code{geometry} holding WKB (\code{BLOB}, as in views
in arrow mode) or of type \code{GEOMETRY} (as in native views) makes the
dataset geospatial, and \code{crs} is then required. The geometries pass
through R in batches: for each batch the bounding boxes, the H3 cells
and the index rows are computed as for an \code{sf} object (with
\code{geocode_engine}, and the warning of "Geocoding engines") and kept in
the staging folder; the rows are then sorted by cell and written from
DuckDB. \code{partition = "h3"} and \code{geocode = FALSE} work as for an \code{sf}
object.
\item Lazy tables on other databases abort: collect them first.
}
}

\section{Geocoding engines}{

A geospatial row whose geometry lies inside one H3 cell gets that cell.
For a row that crosses cell edges, the package computes the cells it
touches and the share of the row in each; \code{h3} is the cell of the largest
share, and every touched cell goes to the index table. \code{geocode_engine}
says how these cells are computed:
\itemize{
\item \code{"auto"} (the default): each such row is checked on the sphere (s2).
A valid row is geocoded on the sphere. An invalid one (for example
valid in the plane, but with a duplicated vertex or a spike that s2
rejects) is rebuilt by s2; when the rebuilt copy is empty or does not
keep its area (length for lines) within
\code{getOption("rcs.s2_rebuild_tol")} (default \code{1e-3}), the row is geocoded
in the plane instead.
\item \code{"s2"}: on the sphere; invalid rows are rebuilt by s2 (geocoded in the
plane when the rebuild fails).
\item \code{"planar"}: every crossing row in the plane of the coordinates
(slower).
}

Rows whose box spans 180 degrees of longitude or more are geocoded on the
sphere with every engine. Rebuilt geometries are used only for the
cells; the stored geometry is unchanged. One warning per write (class
\code{rcs_warning_s2_rebuild}) counts the rows rebuilt and those geocoded in
the plane, and names the first five of each by key (or \code{.rcs_id}). The
engine is recorded with the dataset, and the exact test of
\code{\link[=store_read]{store_read()}} and \code{\link[=store_intersects]{store_intersects()}} follows it by default
(\code{exact_engine}). The same input always gives the same cells, shares and
version. Data in a projected CRS are geocoded from a copy in EPSG:4326.
}

\section{Modes}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"create"}: the dataset must not exist yet.
\item \code{"replace"}: the whole dataset is replaced by \code{x}; columns, key,
partitioning and kind may change. A dataset that does not exist yet is
created.
\item \code{"update"}: for partitioned datasets only. The partitions whose values
occur in \code{x} are replaced entirely by the rows of \code{x}; the other
partitions are kept; new values add partitions. The columns and their
types must equal the stored ones, and the key must stay unique across
the whole dataset. A message lists every replaced partition with its old
and new number of rows: rows of a replaced partition that are missing
from \code{x} disappear. In this mode \code{key}, \code{partition}, \code{h3_res},
\code{geocode} and \code{geocode_engine} come from the stored dataset; arguments
that disagree abort. \code{partition} may be left out; if given, it must be
what was given on create: \code{"h3"}, or the column names. Partition columns
given by the user, even ones named \verb{h3_p<r>}, are kept as given: never
recomputed from the geometries.
With \code{partition = "h3"}, an update that would need more than
\code{max_files} files re-partitions the whole dataset at a coarser H3
resolution.
}

Writing the same data again gives the same version: the call reports the
dataset as unchanged, uploads nothing to the primary, and only brings
secondary locations that differ up to date (none for a primary-only
write).
}

\section{Primary-only datasets}{

\code{locations = "primary"} writes the dataset to the primary location only:
no secondary is landed or even contacted, and each gets the status
\code{"primary only"} in the result (an unavailable secondary too), with no
warning and without counting as behind. The setting is recorded with the
dataset (\code{primary_only} in its catalog entry and manifest, also for its
index table) and kept by later writes that leave \code{locations} out, of any
mode, until \code{locations = "all"} writes the dataset to every location
again. A secondary that already holds the dataset is left as it is: a
message names it and how to update it (\code{store_sync(st, datasets = )}).

Where a dataset is kept does not change its files or its version: the
setting is not written into the data files, so the same data written with
and without it give the same version, and switching it for unchanged data
changes only the catalog entry and sends no data file to the primary.
Switching back with \code{locations = "all"} also copies the dataset to the
secondaries that lack it.

Elsewhere a primary-only dataset is treated thus:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=store_status]{store_status()}}, \code{\link[=store_datasets]{store_datasets()}} and the print of the connection
(symbol \code{P}) report \code{"primary only"}, not \code{"missing"}, for a secondary
that does not hold it;
\item \code{\link[=store_sync]{store_sync()}} does not copy it to a secondary that does not hold it,
also with \code{all = TRUE}; \code{store_sync(st, datasets = "<name>")} and
\code{\link[=store_copy]{store_copy()}} copy it, and a secondary that holds it is refreshed by a
plain \code{store_sync()} like any dataset it holds;
\item \code{\link[=store_read]{store_read()}} does not copy it to a local secondary that lacks it,
whatever its size, unless \code{copy = TRUE}.
}

Use it for data that should stay in the primary, typically a large raster
in a bucket that is read a window at a time straight from there.
}

\section{History}{

Whether the replaced version is kept is decided by the dataset's own
setting (\code{history}, recorded in its catalog entry), else by the
connection's (\code{store_connect(history = )}), else by the store's default
(\code{\link[=store_create]{store_create()}}). Without history, the files of the replaced version
are deleted right after the catalogs are switched; with history, they stay
until \code{\link[=store_prune]{store_prune()}} removes them.
}

\section{Failures}{

Every transfer is tried up to three times. If the primary fails before
its catalog is switched, the call aborts and no catalog has changed;
uploaded files are reused when the call is repeated (and removed by
\code{\link[=store_prune]{store_prune()}} otherwise). If a secondary fails, the call warns,
continues with the next location and reports the secondary as behind;
\code{store_sync(st, datasets = "<name>")} repairs it, or
\code{store_sync(st, all = TRUE)} for every dataset (a plain \code{store_sync()}
refreshes only the datasets a secondary already holds).
}

\examples{
root <- file.path(tempdir(), "rcs-example-write")
st <- store_connect(
  file.path(root, "primary"),
  secondary = file.path(root, "copy"),
  create = TRUE
)
sales <- rcs_sim_table(200, seed = 1)
store_write(st, sales, "sales/daily",
  title = "Daily sales",
  description = "Simulated daily sales by shop and product.",
  key = c("shop_id", "date", "product"), partition = "year"
)

# The same data again: nothing is uploaded
store_write(st, sales, "sales/daily",
  title = "Daily sales",
  description = "Simulated daily sales by shop and product.",
  key = c("shop_id", "date", "product"), partition = "year",
  mode = "replace"
)

# Replace one year
year_2024 <- sales[sales$year == 2024, ]
year_2024$qty <- year_2024$qty + 1L
store_write(st, year_2024, "sales/daily",
  title = "Daily sales",
  description = "Simulated daily sales by shop and product.",
  mode = "update"
)
st

# A class map: the codes go into the file, the legend and the colours
# into the catalog. `nodata = 0`: cells holding 0 are outside the map.
if (requireNamespace("terra", quietly = TRUE)) {
  cover <- terra::as.int(rcs_sim_raster(nrow = 60, ncol = 80)[["cover"]])
  cover[1:2, ] <- 0L
  levels(cover) <- data.frame(
    value = 1:5,
    label = c("water", "forest", "crop", "grass", "bare")
  )
  names(cover) <- "cover" # `levels<-` renamed the layer
  terra::coltab(cover) <- data.frame(
    value = 1:5,
    col = c("#1F78B4", "#33A02C", "#FFD92F", "#B2DF8A", "#B15928")
  )
  store_write(st, cover, "grids/cover",
    title = "Land cover", description = "A simulated class map.",
    nodata = 0
  )
  # One byte per cell, the NoData value and the number of classes
  print(store_describe(st, "grids/cover")$columns)

  # A large map is best written straight from its file: the data type
  # and the NoData tag of the file are kept. `gdal` changes the
  # compression here.
  file <- file.path(root, "cover.tif")
  terra::writeRaster(cover, file, datatype = "INT1U", NAflag = 0)
  store_write(st, terra::rast(file), "grids/cover_zstd",
    title = "Land cover, ZSTD", description = "A simulated class map.",
    gdal = c("COMPRESS=ZSTD", "LEVEL=9")
  )
  # The label column is named `cover` here, not `label`:
  # `terra::writeRaster()` named it after the layer
  print(terra::levels(store_read(st, "grids/cover_zstd"))[[1]])
}
unlink(root, recursive = TRUE)
}
